From 53185cf7cb4262f8a696fc7f5a9780faf9cf60e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:46:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/17] feat(gateway): propagate W3C trace context and log it
 with the ids

Requests crossing the gateway could only be correlated through the
correlation id, which inference instances and tracing tools do not
understand. The gateway now keeps exactly one valid inbound W3C
traceparent (with its tracestate) or otherwise starts a new unsampled
trace and drops tracestate. The resulting header is forwarded to the
control plane, to resolver lookups and to inference instances, so the
control plane logs and error envelopes carry the same trace id. The
gateway records no spans, so a valid header is passed on unchanged;
OTLP export is left as a follow-up.

To make the ids visible where operators look, every /api/v1 request
now gets one access log line with method, path, status, duration,
correlation id and trace id. It sits before authentication and rate
limiting, so rejected requests are logged too, and records 500 when a
handler panics before writing a response. Gateway error envelopes gain
an optional traceId, matching the control plane's Error schema.

The stream proxy now writes its errors through middleware.WriteError,
so they also carry correlationId, and its logs read the correlation id
from the request context instead of a hard-coded X-Correlation-ID
header, which was wrong when GATEWAY_CORRELATION_HEADER is customised.
